le story from start to finish yeah  how much is possible how much is  possible but  how much damage is being done every  single day  when you allow people to put a tent up  and kill themselves and when we don't  talk about is how many dead people there  are in all of these tents that you see  everywhere  because people overdose and they die and  no one goes in the tents  and um that's so inhumane  so i had a lot of pushback when i was  hold on  forcing amanda to go to treatment but  again  amanda could not make her own decision  that was the only way because amanda was  ever gonna get better  was forcing her yeah but those but  people who  who have that much damage to their brain  from drug use and also the trauma that  she suffered like  dying in dignity does not mean let her  go be free she's an  adult that the amount of damage she took  to just her skull and her jaw and all  the lost  teeth that she lost um  is it's just unbelievable that that  our system would consider letting you  put a tent  up as humane and that it's okay i feel  like  there should be a lot done to change the  homelessness situation and offer  more help to people and  i actually spoke with uh meet kevin  who's  who's running for governor and i have an  interview with him live stream on his  channel  on tuesday at 2 pm pst um he's talking  to me about how we could restructure  the homelessness epidemic and mental  health epidemic  in the united states in california  specifically  and then hopefully people can mimic that  plan for other states  the key to amanda's recovery i think was  the forced  rehab yeah it was forced she didn't she  didn't have a choice of  whether she wanted to stay here exactly  because we literally pulled her rock  bottom because  if an addict is given a choice  eventually they'll have a bad day and  leave  yeah and i believe in what you say  possibilities all things are possible as  a result  of what has happened to amanda because i  believe i pray every day and believe  in my prayers and i believe that god has  helped us in this  brought her us together and he just  showed us that what we did was  all good for us no his amazing story  that amanda  recovered fully unbelievably yeah  she claimed it repeatedly yeah it was  beautiful  yeah she made sure and larry your your  strength throughout this whole week  has been incredible well on your drive  on your drive to las vegas um to go see  amanda um you saw that there was an  endless rainbow oh wow i have never seen  this and i used to take pictures around  the world of places sunset  sunrise we were traveling on the back  road  to las vegas and we had a wrap-around  rainbow  and when i saw it i'm going well okay  first we see everything in california  beautiful weather all the time  but once i kept looking and i never saw  the end of it  and i began to pray because i'm going to  prepare to see amanda  i've never seen a wraparound rainbow  before  albert kept saying you're right  you know albert he's missed a curiosity  we had nothing to say it was just  incredible and it didn't go away didn't  fade away quickly  no it was um i wrote i wrote something  down because it really stuck with me  yeah so um i was telling one of  our friends about what happened and he  kind of made like a nerdy  affiliation it was a starbucks uh star  wars quote  but it really resonated with amanda so  he said that there was a scene in star  wars where the bad guys were fighting  and where the good guy was fighting the  bad guy and the good guy  said um you can't win  even if you strike me down i will become  sorry  i will become more powerful  than you could ever imagine  and i feel like amanda's death  is gonna change the entire way that we  do things  but more importantly amanda's life  and what she proved while she was alive  um i went from planning her future to  her funeral  it's  but amanda's favorite song was it's a  wonderful world  i used to play that song when she was in  the car she just to make her keep quiet  and i would look back and she would be  just into the song  man so i would just play it again so it  turned out that she would always want me  to play  when we returned back from las vegas  there was a man  standing um we were dropping larry home  and there was a man standing outside of  larry's building with his dog  and he heard what we were talking about  amanda and i guess he had known amanda  as well  and he just said they say that god  takes you when you've served your  purpose  but i just thought that that was so  powerful and i think that  um you know we all have so much to learn  from her short life  but i'm really grateful that i met  amanda i'm really grateful that we got  to work together  and i'm really grateful that um larry is  going to continue  to work with me and build up amanda's  legacy and prove that  you know not let her die in vain but  really  use what she proved while she was alive  that anything is possible  to transform um so many lives so  we're taking down the gofundme page  because she's no longer here  um but if anyone wants to continue to  support what we're doing  with offering scholarships and trying to  make it open and accessible  to everyone you can sign the petition  and you can also donate directly on the  website it goes  to giving and we want to open up the  scholarship program to  everyone applicants once we raise enough  funding  to be able to help everybody well i  really see so clearly because i've  helped  tried to help so many people on my own  and the money ends up being wasted  because as i see it the only way that  getting someone clean off drugs is is a  program similar to what amanda had  yeah which is which was two two months  of  forced jail time formula it's either  jail or psych ward  or you can willingly go to a homeless  shelter  i used to volunteer here on skid row i  had my birthday party here on skid row  at the la mission  um i was when i first got here and i  witnessed what was going on  i thought oh my god these people  probably don't know where to find help  and when i arrived i  i realized that it's actually  skid row is just the entire block  you've got four different homeless  shelters  but the problem is when we'd be cooking  um in the  in the kitchen we would have so  much food and so little people come in  that we'd be serving seconds and thirds  and all of the people are just sitting  outside they refuse to come in and the  programs aren't  forcing you to do a program they're not  forcing you to be clean so you can  actually  come in eat and then leave  you have the second choice of coming in  eating and staying for one night but not  committing to a program and then you can  also come  in eat stay for an entire program and  then they help do job placement  and all of that other stuff and they  also offer mental health resources  i mean getting you clean off of drugs  but the problem is people will not come  in not even to eat a meal  and it's because again their brain is so  damaged you took away the prefrontal  cortex you took away their logic  and when you look at brain activity  everything  shuts down when someone's so  malnutritioned  all the lights and all the other parts  of the brain the blood flow  stops flowing it just goes to the center  and that's survival mode so the only  thing they're thinking of is drug  seeking what's going to make me feel  better right now  and they cannot plan for the future  because that's prefrontal cortex  and any sort of motivation is located in  the pleasure center so like the  right and left hemispheres of the brain  which is where all the dopamine goes  so it's like the motivation is only  there when they're high and it leaves  when they're not  so you can't expect those people to make  decisions for themselves  so something needs to change yeah  absolutely  let's hope amanda's beautiful story  isn't in vain and leaving this is not  the end this is only the beginning  yeah larry and lima thank you so much  thank you mark  thank you mark you've been a treasure  man we'll carry on  thank you thank you thank you  you
 

